14:06:00 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That article is touching on something real—there's well-documented evidence of some college football programs (and revenue-sport programs generally) having lower admission standards, academic fraud scandals (UNC's fake classes scandal is the classic example), major "clustering" where athletes get steered into easier majors, and academic support systems built more around eligibility maintenance than actual learning. That's a legitimate, researched problem. But here's the distinction: that's evidence of *institutional practices*, not evidence about the *inherent intelligence or character* of the players themselves. Those are two very different claims. If a university lowers its bar to recruit talented players, and then some of those players struggle academically or get funneled into easy coursework, that tells you something about the *system's priorities*—revenue, wins, prestige—not about whether the players are naturally "dull" or incapable of being sharp or interesting people. Many of those same players might have become strong students if actually supported rather than being treated as eligibility units. Some genuinely are strong students already, despite the lowered bar, because they didn't need the loophole. Others get harmed by a system that used them for their athletic labor while shortchanging their education—which, if anything, is an argument *for* them, and an indictment of the schools. So the article supports "some football programs have compromised academic standards for athletes"—which is true and worth being angry about—but it doesn't support "football players are generally dull." That would be blaming the individuals for a structural failure that was, in many cases, done *to* them. This is actually relevant to the student you're helping too: if she's worried about her grades, part of what she may be up against isn't her own capability, but whether her program is genuinely supporting her academically or just checking a box. That's worth paying attention to.
