14:11:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Fair enough — quick thought and then let's get practical: hanging out with teammates during downtime is just normal social behavior for anyone in a tight-knit group with a shared schedule, not evidence of a "type." College students in general default to socializing when they have unstructured time — that's not football-specific. But I'll leave it there. For the midday block, the key is making it *structured enough* that it doesn't default to hangout time by inertia, without making it feel like punishment. A few ways to do that: **1. Reframe it as "半 social, half work"** - She doesn't have to isolate from teammates to use the time well. Studying alongside teammates (even if not the same subject) can work — body double effect. If a couple of them are willing to sit together and each do their own work for 45-60 min, it's more sustainable than trying to solo-discipline her way out of a group habit. **2. Set a specific trigger** - Instead of "I'll study sometime midday," something concrete like: "After lunch, 1:00-2:00, I'm doing homework" — same time daily, so it becomes automatic rather than a decision she has to remake every day (decision fatigue is real, especially when tired) **3. Start small, not heroic** - If she's currently using zero of that block for work, jumping to "protect the whole 2.5 hours" will probably fail. Better to carve out one reliable hour first, prove to herself it's sustainable, then expand if needed. **4. Match task to energy** - Right after lunch, energy dips (post-meal slump) — so maybe lighter tasks first (reviewing notes, reading) then move to harder tasks (writing, problem sets) once she's more alert, closer to 1:30-2. **5. Remove friction** - Have she and any teammates who also want to use the time pick one consistent spot (library corner, empty classroom) so it's not a constant negotiation or distraction-heavy environment (dorm common room, etc.) Do you know if she has any teammates who are already academically disciplined that she could partner with for this block? Having even one accountability partner tends to make this stick a lot better than trying to solo it against the pull of the group.
