14:11:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Here are a few versions depending on her style and how well she knows the person — pick whichever fits her voice best, or mix and match: **Casual/direct:** > "Hey, I'm trying to actually get work done during our midday gap instead of just chilling the whole time lol. Want to grab lunch and then hit the library with me for like an hour before practice? No pressure, just trying to stay on top of stuff this season." **Lower-key, low-commitment ask:** > "Random ask — I'm trying to be better about using our free block to study instead of just hanging out. Would you want to sit together and both just knock out some work? Doesn't have to be the same class, just having someone else doing it too helps me actually focus." **If it's someone she knows is already stressed about grades:** > "I know you mentioned you're behind on [class/assignment] — want to team up and use the midday block to actually grind through some work? I need the accountability too tbh." **Group version (if she wants to loop in more than one person):** > "Thinking about starting a lunch + study thing during our gap before practice — like an hour, library or somewhere quiet, everyone doing their own work. Anyone want in? Trying to actually use that time instead of losing it every day lol." A couple tips for however she sends it: - Keep it light and practical, not preachy — "trying to actually get stuff done" lands better than anything that sounds like she's judging how others use their time - Suggesting a specific plan (lunch → library → set time) makes it easier for the other person to say yes, versus a vague "we should study sometime" - If the first person says no or flakes, no big deal — she can just ask someone else or default to solo body-doubling with headphones Want me to also put together a simple weekly template she could use to track whether the block is actually happening, so it's easy to see if the habit's sticking?
