14:12:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Here's a simple weekly tracker she can use — low-effort enough that she'll actually fill it out, but enough structure to show patterns over time. --- **WEEKLY STUDY BLOCK TRACKER** | Day | Block Happened? (Y/N) | Time Used (mins) | What I worked on | Energy Level (1-5) | Notes | |-----|------------------------|-------------------|--------------------|----------------------|-------| | Mon | | | | | | | Tue | | | | | | | Wed | | | | | | | Thu | | | | | | | Fri | | | | | | | Sat (game?) | | | | | | | Sun | | | | | | **End of week check-in (2 minutes, Sunday night):** 1. How many days did the block actually happen? ___ / 7 2. Did having a study partner help, hurt, or not matter? 3. What got in the way on days it didn't happen? (practice ran long / too tired / partner flaked / forgot / other) 4. One thing to adjust for next week: --- **Why this works:** - **Y/N + minutes** makes it dead simple to see the pattern at a glance — no need to overthink entries - **Energy level** helps identify if certain days are consistently bad (e.g., always skips Thursdays because Wednesday practice is brutal) so the schedule can adapt instead of forcing something that isn't realistic - **"What got in the way"** turns missed days into data instead of guilt — the goal is adjusting the plan, not self-criticism - **One weekly adjustment** keeps it from becoming a static rulebook; the schedule should flex around football's actual demands as the season goes She could keep this in a notes app, a physical planner, or even a simple recurring note — whatever's lowest friction for her to actually update daily. If she skips a day of tracking, no big deal, she just picks back up next day. Want me to also sketch out a version specifically for game weeks (Thu-Sun disrupted by travel) so she has a slightly different template for those weeks vs. normal weeks?
